@anon.a2b023d1a4e2834952d16152dce23780.1301 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [972 x i8] }> <{ [972 x i8] c"A sequence of values.\0A\0AYou can construct an array by enclosing a comma-separated sequence of values\0Ain parentheses. The values do not have to be of the same type.\0A\0AYou can access and update array items with the `.at()` method. Indices are\0Azero-based and negative indices wrap around to the end of the array. You can\0Aiterate over an array using a [for loop]($scripting/#loops). Arrays can be\0Aadded together with the `+` operator, [joined together]($scripting/#blocks)\0Aand multiplied with integers.\0A\0A**Note:** An array of length one needs a trailing comma, as in `{(1,)}`.\0AThis is to disambiguate from a simple parenthesized expressions like `{(1 +\0A2) * 3}`. An empty array is written as `{()}`.\0A\0A# Example\0A```example\0A#let values = (1, 7, 4, -3, 2)\0A\0A#values.at(0) \\\0A#(values.at(0) = 3)\0A#values.at(-1) \\\0A#values.find(calc.even) \\\0A#values.filter(calc.odd) \\\0A#values.map(calc.abs) \\\0A#values.rev() \\\0A#(1, (2, 3)).flatten() \\\0A#((\22A\22, \22B\22, \22C\22)\0A    .join(\22, \22, last: \22 and \22))\0A```" }>, align 1
